---
scope: src/service/install/scaffoldReadme
status: aligned
description: "Install category that ensures the host project has a project-root README with the AIDE adoption badge visible at the top."
intent: >
  scaffoldReadme is the install category that makes AIDE adoption
  visible to anyone who lands on the host project. When no README
  exists, it scaffolds an authored-feeling first draft with the AIDE
  badge already in place; when one exists, it weaves the badge into
  the top so the project's README announces AIDE alongside whatever
  other tooling badges are already there. Re-running the install or
  upgrade leaves an already-badged README untouched and never
  recreates an existing one.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The AIDE badge ends up at the top of the visible README content, joining any existing badge strip rather than displacing it, and never landing inside or before the document's leading metadata block.
    - When the host project has no README, the category produces a first draft that reads as authored — a recognizable project name and a short placeholder line — not a templated skeleton a human would immediately delete.
    - Re-running the category against an already-badged README is a no-op and never produces a second AIDE badge, regardless of cosmetic edits the user has made to the existing badge's surrounding markdown.
    - An existing README is never overwritten, truncated, or recreated by the create path; the create and inject paths are mutually exclusive on every run.
  undesired:
    - A duplicate AIDE badge after re-run because detection keyed on cosmetic markdown around the badge instead of the badge's stable identity.
    - A generated README that reads as machine-produced — generic placeholder names, tautological description lines, or bare headings with no content signal.
    - The badge inserted below the first heading, inside the leading metadata block, or above it in a position that breaks the document's metadata parsing.
    - Divergent behavior between the install and upgrade paths against the same project state, so a user sees the badge appear on one path and silently not appear on the other.
---
